{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective","id":531432,"title":"A new perspective","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-05-28T17:46:11-07:00","id":531432,"title":"A new perspective","content":"While I was making some food and thinking of vampires (I can't be the only one), I recalled the clan buff in Danse Macabre for the Venture that gave them a bonus to political rolls. As a simulationist, I like the mechanics to be thematically present. Ergo, I wondered how this could be. From this, I formed the notion that, just as the default beast would urge one to consume those nearby and flee the sun, each clan had a different beast. Not so radically different to be a different genus, but a new species.<br /> <br /> I'm curious if anyone has any ideas of how to furnish this idea? How can I thematically convey the character of each clan as if it was the Beast's urging.<br /> <br /> I've to go but I'll check in soon to see what people have come up with. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T18:19:14-07:00","id":531474,"title":"","content":"This is pretty much canon, actually. At least, as canon as anything can be in nWoD. It's very strongly implied that the Clans are all different kinds of vampiric creature that have grown more and more similar over time through something akin to convergent evolution. This is supported by vastly different origin stories for the different Clans, and a few clans even having multiple independent origins, as well as at least one doccumented example of a group of non-Kindred vampires (specifically, the Jiang-Shi population in The Triangle in North Carolina) steadily growing more Kindred-like, to the point that the Triangle's Queen actually grants them official Clan status within her domain.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T20:13:31-07:00","id":531598,"title":"","content":"Oh right. When it comes to these things, I tend to need more than implication to get the point. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I've always just played the Kindred as based on an archetypical vampire and supposed that the clans were simply hyper-powered bloodlines, a sort of bloodline ascended. I think I've noticed the hinting of different origins but thought they were more myth, whereas the metaphysical reality was much simpler: Mage is correct and there is a platonic vampire type that all vampires indirectly partake in. :P<br /> <br /> However, if vampires are different in kind, then .... well, that does legitimizes my search. As such, my invite to come up with inner-beast dynamics still stands. Yay!<br /> <br /> As a side note: convergent evolution, does that actually occur in nature?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T20:55:59-07:00","id":531684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tabanese</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=531598#post531598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh right. When it comes to these things, I tend to need more than implication to get the point. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I've always just played the Kindred as based on an archetypical vampire and supposed that the clans were simply hyper-powered bloodlines, a sort of bloodline ascended. I think I've noticed the hinting of different origins but thought they were more myth, whereas the metaphysical reality was much simpler: Mage is correct and there is a platonic vampire type that all vampires indirectly partake in. :P</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a pretty cool interpretation for a mage-centric game, for sure!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tabanese</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=531598#post531598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, if vampires are different in kind, then .... well, that does legitimizes my search. As such, my invite to come up with inner-beast dynamics still stands. Yay</div> </div> </div> </div>Rejoice! Although, I'm not entirely sure I completely understand what you're looking for. Ways to mechanically represent each Clan's particular variations on the Beast? I mean, there's the obvious answer that that's what the various Clan Banes, Disciplines, and exclusive Merits are for, but somehow I doubt that's the answer you wanted... I'd say if you wanted to add some custom mechanics, the best way might be to add some riders onto the Lash Out mechanics based on Clan.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tabanese</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=531598#post531598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a side note: convergent evolution, does that actually occur in nature?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, all the time. Flight is a good example, it's evolved like four or five times completely independently throughout history. Insects, pterosaurs, birds, and flying mammals, just off the top of my head. Obviously, things work a little differently with vampires since the supernatural is involved. The similarities between the various kinds of vampires probably come more from the fact that they're all reanimated human corpses and the fact that they've been literally feeding on each other's animating vital energy for millenia than it has to do with natural and sexual selection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 05-28-2015, 11:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T02:09:07-07:00","id":531806,"title":"","content":"This is complicated somewhat by how the gangrel and ventrue <i>might </i>be offshoots of the same thing, along with the julii. And then there's the strix, which may or may not be behind one or more clans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T08:12:25-07:00","id":531962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=531806#post531806\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is complicated somewhat by how the gangrel and ventrue <i>might </i>be offshoots of the same thing, along with the julii. And then there's the strix, which may or may not be behind one or more clans.</div> </div> </div> </div>And of course there's Clan Grettir.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T10:48:10-07:00","id":532047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=531806#post531806\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is complicated somewhat by how the gangrel and ventrue <i>might </i>be offshoots of the same thing, along with the julii. And then there's the strix, which may or may not be behind one or more clans.</div> </div> </div> </div>Some of them might be, while others might not. I mean, it's been hinted that some clans may have multiple origins, so maybe some Ventrue are Gangrel/Julii offshoots, while others are something completely different.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T11:59:19-07:00","id":532103,"title":"","content":"Heck, in B&amp;S, it is suggested that both are offshoots of each other iirc.<br /> <br /> But let's keep it simple for now. Five clans, origins not of the highest importance. I wasn't looking for mechanical buffs but as with everything, to each their own. I was interested in simply discussing the experience of each Beast, assuming each beast had different urgings. Kinda like how each mage sight comes in several flavors.<br /> <br /> I've two examples I think hammer home this point.<br /> <br /> The first is just looking at the Beast in its raw form. To do this, we simply have to look at the Draugr, as it is a Beast without Man. As it is in RAW, it seems that all vampires as Draugr have the same routine and this implies that they behave the same. What I'm suggesting and want to develop is that each clan has a specific Beastly aspect. If vampires share a common source, clans diverge as much as bloodlines. If not, this difference can be fundamental. What is important is is the idea that a Draugr represents the archetype of each clan. While Gangrel and Nosferatu Draugr might look like the type suggested by RAW (viciously stalks and kills its living relations), a Mekhet would be a shadow stalker, a Deava would lure and ensnare, and a Venture would brood and trap. When there is the Man, these preferences creep out. It is why so many Venture become manipulative and political; it is their beastly desire. Variation can occur, of course, but the Beast acts as a prism for your undead experience and the strength of this prism is the inverse related to the strength of your humanity.<br /> <br /> With a perfect humanity, each vampire is an individual. Peter the Deava can enjoy fishing by the moonlight and Liza the Venture is a supportive person in the lives of those closest. However, as the humanity drops, the Beast colors their experience. Fishing ceases to be meaningful and more vigorous pursuits are needed. Support and modesty turn to dominion and pride. What I am trying to suggest is that the Beast has a viewpoint and within the union of Man and Beast that is a vampire, this viewpoint needs to be expressed to the player. The ST needs to remind Peter that his daughters play is a trivial and boring affair, while he reminds Liza that those around her incompetent morons. Indeed, with low enough humanity, the ST shouldn't bother with an objective perspective. Don't say 'As you try to accept your sisters apology for being late, the Beast spits at her weakness', say 'Your sister is late. Again. She seems to curl up under your gaze and timidly makes a flimsy excuse even she knows is false.'<br /> <br /> My second example is how I made sense of this while thinking about it. For those who know the game, this will seem vague but hopefully, that just makes it approachable for those who don't. Suppose five League of Legend gamers are embraced to the different clans. As their preferred past-time is an online video game, they use it as an escape from the unpredictability of their new life. However, though the Beast wouldn't play League normally, it is chained to the whims of the Man as much as the Man to it. As such, the players notice they now play different. The Deava suddenly finds it an easy affair to chain deadly combos together and out-play opponents. What they intend to do and what they do now line up with frequent regularity. The Gangrel finds himself pulling back from an attack moments before an ambush, and knowing -just knowing- which path to take. His survivability shoots up. He also finds himself preferring tanks and melee. The Nosferatu starts to play with his opposition. He just knows which champion and items will give his opponent a hard time and how to play up this advantage. He also starts to use chat to infuriate the enemy team, unnerving daring advances and splitting defense against shadows. The Mekhet starts to notice tells and give aways. He always seems to be aware of cooldowns and mana reserves of the enemy, as well as deriving the location of others from small and subtle details. The water splashing through fog of war. The bush moving. The enemy suddenly having dragon buffs. It is as if the game is an open book. The Venture stops playing a personal game and develops a wider perspective. He never plays against opponents who defend properly anymore; there is always a weak spot. Additionally, he rarely goes a game without directing his team. Previously, he only spoke to them to badmouth their preformence but now, he instructs and his instructions pay off. After a few minutes, he has his team coordinated in a way usually reserved for the pros. And that's in solo que.<br /> <br /> Okay, second example got away from me but it is basically the first in different garb. I'm going to cease writing now before this wall of text makes the Chinese envious. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T12:35:04-07:00","id":532119,"title":"","content":"I like differentiating clans in terms of how they experience hunger. <br /> <br /> With the Serpents, it's an intimate, almost sexual thing - you desire to be close to specific people, feel their touch, share your emotions and your pain (all Daeva know pain). You need a connection, and the best way to find that is the Kiss - the perfect expression of physical intimacy. <br /> <br /> The Savages are the most honest clan in that their hunger is just that - hunger. A burning desire to feed, to gorge yourself in the blood of prey you have hunted down yourself using nothing but your own claws. <br /> <br /> The Shadows grow cold and dark and silent when the're hungry. And there are all these whispering voices, telling them to do bad shit, and they get louder and more insistent and demanding as a Mekhet gets hungrier and colder, and sooner or later you'll have no choice but to do what they tell you. <br /> <br /> The Haunts are monsters. To be Nesuferitul is to be part of a lineage that is inherently inhuman and removed from all things natural and pure - far more so than all other vampires. <br /> It gets lonely being a monster - lonely and scary. The only way to make it stop is to try and be a little more human - and to do that, you need to have something human inside you. <br /> <br /> The Lords experience their hunger as a desire to assert their dominance over other people. Lords are patriarchial abusers who cannot help but feel the strong need to lift themselves up by putting others down. The Ventrue Beast tells them that others must obey; and what better way to put them into their place than by feeding on them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T12:41:59-07:00","id":532122,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Christian A</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=532119#post532119\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> The only way to make it stop is to try and be a little more human - and to do that, you need to have something human inside you.</div> </div> </div> </div>This line is a great example of irrational explanation: I understand but don't. :P<br /> <br /> This is the kind of thing I was wondering about. I likely won't make that the only aspect of Kindred phenomenology but as a part, definitely. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-01T01:32:48-07:00","id":535365,"title":"","content":"The clan books are actually really good for this kind of thing; they're written as if they were in-universe documents, and focus a lot on the first-hand experience of being a member of a given clan. For instance, the in-universe compiler of the Gangrel book is tormented by vivid and visceral dreams, in some of which she seems to actually talk to a personification of her beast, while the compiler of the Mekhet book compares succumbing to the beast to becoming like a clockwork doll, bereft of both thought and emotion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 06-01-2015, 04:37 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-01T09:49:34-07:00","id":535687,"title":"","content":"There's several origin stories floating around, with lots of different possibilities. No one's mentioned the &quot;we all come from strix!&quot; one, though.<br /> <br /> <br /> But, to answer the OP: Irregardless of clan origins, there are still several methods to differentiate the clans. With the new Blood and Smoke book, my favorite way has been to play up both the clan banes, and make the &quot;clan merits&quot; very prevalent among the clan's NPCs, and giving the PCs the option of getting them for free at char gen. Interestingly enough, the four merits in the core book just kind of happened without planning - convergent evolution of merits! After the book came out, people asked for a Ventrue one - I don't remember where it is, but one of the writers made one up somewhere... Ah, found it!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MachineIV</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/106196-ventrue-specific-merit?p=107577#post107577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Heart of the Challenger (\u2022\u2022)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b> Ventrue<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Your Lord's Beast stands as a gargoyle perched upon a parapet, ready to strike against any that would assail her. When any character unleashes the Predatory Aura on your character, that character gains the Competitive Condition, unless he achieves an Exceptional Success on the roll. If he already has the Competitive Condition, it becomes Persistent, and can only be resolved by besting your character in a competition where the character lost a Humanity/Integrity dot.<br /> Once per scene, you can take +3 dice against any single character with the Competitive Condition, as if you'd spent a point of Willpower. You cannot spend Willpower to add additional dice to such a roll. This counts toward the Competitive Condition's requirement for spending Willpower.<br /> Note that this Merit supersedes the normal monthly limitation on the Competitive Condition.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> Your character suffers the Competitive Condition at all times. It cannot be resolved. However, when it would normally be resolved, gain a Beat.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> So, there you go. Merits and banes to emphasize clan. I also have seen people make the clan signature disciplines into clan-unique disciplines to help. I like keeping things simple, and those are the best way I know how.<br /> <br /> You can also steal the ghoul clan-specific merits and give them to full vamps, I suppose, which could help, though only do that in a ghoul-less game, I'd say.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 06-01-2015, 12:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-01T16:37:12-07:00","id":536119,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CatDoom</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=535365#post535365\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The clan books are actually really good for this kind of thing; they're written as if they were in-universe documents, and focus a lot on the first-hand experience of being a member of a given clan. For instance, the in-universe compiler of the Gangrel book is tormented by vivid and visceral dreams, in some of which she seems to actually talk to a personification of her beast, while the compiler of the Mekhet book compares succumbing to the beast to becoming like a clockwork doll, bereft of both thought and emotion.</div> </div> </div> </div>I love the idea that Gangrel are so close to their Beast that it has a voice and torments them in an overt way. This would definitely change one's night to night. Why are Mekhet beast's robotic though?<br /> <br /> @MCN<br /> <br /> This is awesome for conveying other peoples internal logic to the player. I'll have to look for those other clan merits. In addition, I want to convey the world to my players as if from their subjective perspective. Basing that on the clan merits is a viable route; indeed, Venture are the easiest for me to imagine and it matches that merit. Just make every other persons effort seem pathetic or threatening and by consistently laying it on, hopefully make the PLAYER act like a Venture. I normally play pretty one-on-one, so this would be easy but in a group I imagine it would be hysterical.<br /> <br /> Example:<br /> A Venture and a Non-Venture get cards each. The cards both describe a person who just walked into the bar. The Venture sees a shady, spy-like individual attempting to get close to those around them. The Non-Venture sees a person asking for directions to the train station. :P<br /> <br /> I'd like the strength of this lens to be the inverse of humanity strength. So a highly grounded Venture might only see a person who was foolish and should know better. A low humanity Venture however would almost cease to see people who they didn't consider weaklings. Almost everyone, with maybe the exception of an attached touchstone, would speak with a grovelling tone and stand meekly, constantly acting in an anxious manner. Hence why Venture find it easy to detach from humanity; it's fucking pathetic from their perspective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2003,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","user":"Tabanese","date":".  Clarification and ideas. 06-01-2015, 09:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-01T23:23:21-07:00","id":536592,"title":"","content":"I think that the &quot;roboticness&quot; of the Mekhet's beast beast is meant, in part, to simply be that individual vampire's perception of things. That said, it makes sense that the beast of the Mekhet would be colder and less wild than that of other vampires. Thematically, the Mekhet are associated with darkness, silence, and stillness; the ancient Roman Mekhet even rationalized their supernatural perception as stemming from a unique ability to stil their minds and bodies. One wouldn't expect a Mekhet in the throws of her beast to hiss or scream; rather, she might slip into the shadows, striking silently at her prey with a swiftness and economy of motion like that of a striking cobra.<br /> <br /> <br /> With regard to the origins of the clans, I think it's noteworthy that all five clans appear to have been present in the Americas prior to the arrival of Europeans. The Daeva were the dominant clan among the cities of Mesoamerica, but shared the night with the Gangrel and Mekhet. The Nosferatu clanbook contains an account of ancient members of the clan emerging from an unearthed grave in the San Fernando valley, while the second edition core mentions that Montreal hosts a brood of Ventrue that predates European settlement.<br /> <br /> Considering how difficult it is for most vampires to travel long distances, particularly in regions where the kine are few and spread sparsely across the landscape, it seems unlikely that Vampires (except perhaps the Gangrel) could have entered the Americas by the same routes that humans did. The most likely explanation seems to be that the clans somehow arose independently on both sides of the Atlantic.<br /> <br /> One possibility is the Strix, of course; the nemeses are better suited to long-distance travel than the Kindred since they can move about during the day while possessing a host and potentially subsist on a wider variety of prey, like spirits. Using the Synthesis embodiment, a Strix could potentially turn a corpse or a living mortal into a vampire of any clan, which could then embrace others as normal and spread the curse. Other possibilities exist, however; legends claim that corpses can sometimes spontaneously arise as Nosferatu, and there might be some similar process that can turn those denied proper burials into something like a Hollow Mekhet. Other stories claim that the first Gangrel were somehow the product of bestiality, and that the Ventrue were originally household spirits tied to particular families, who were subsequently cursed to become vampires by a goddess.<br /> <br /> Regardless of the truth, it seems to be the case that, wherever mortal humans go, the kindred are never far behind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 06-02-2015, 02:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T00:29:41-07:00","id":536659,"title":"","content":"Predators tend to follow prey. It's not like human migration patterns involved us just marching toward new land. It's a slow thing. We settled somewhere, we spread out, and the population just sort of drifted over the landscape. Easily slow enough for vampires to follow. <br /> <br /> That aside, they might have just walked along the seabed. Deep underwater where there's no sun is where vamps can move with the least hassle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T04:09:12-07:00","id":536789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tabanese</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=532103#post532103\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My second example is how I made sense of this while thinking about it. For those who know the game, this will seem vague but hopefully, that just makes it approachable for those who don't. Suppose five League of Legend gamers are embraced to the different clans. As their preferred past-time is an online video game, they use it as an escape from the unpredictability of their new life. However, though the Beast wouldn't play League normally, it is chained to the whims of the Man as much as the Man to it. As such, the players notice they now play different. The Deava suddenly finds it an easy affair to chain deadly combos together and out-play opponents. What they intend to do and what they do now line up with frequent regularity. The Gangrel finds himself pulling back from an attack moments before an ambush, and knowing -just knowing- which path to take. His survivability shoots up. He also finds himself preferring tanks and melee. The Nosferatu starts to play with his opposition. He just knows which champion and items will give his opponent a hard time and how to play up this advantage. He also starts to use chat to infuriate the enemy team, unnerving daring advances and splitting defense against shadows. The Mekhet starts to notice tells and give aways. He always seems to be aware of cooldowns and mana reserves of the enemy, as well as deriving the location of others from small and subtle details. The water splashing through fog of war. The bush moving. The enemy suddenly having dragon buffs. It is as if the game is an open book. The Venture stops playing a personal game and develops a wider perspective. He never plays against opponents who defend properly anymore; there is always a weak spot. Additionally, he rarely goes a game without directing his team. Previously, he only spoke to them to badmouth their preformence but now, he instructs and his instructions pay off. After a few minutes, he has his team coordinated in a way usually reserved for the pros. And that's in solo qeue.</div> </div> </div> </div>Asa League of Legends player myself, that example came as a shock initially. Ihad to re-read the three first lines two times in order to make myself sure i wasn't just fooling myself over its meaning xD That being said, i've enjoyed your examples very much. Nice and weird, mixing these two hobbies in one message in OnyxPath. <br /> <br /> But let's not forget you're talking about vampires: you have gone and thought about the good things (well, not exactly good in that chat bothering Noseratu... let's say you've focused on the ways being a vampire would improve their playstyle). But now, you have to go back and think about all the wrongness they would inherit, too. They are lonely predators by nature, who weirdly need to stick together as a reminiscence of what they were as the Man - some dying part of themselves- whisper to them. But they have gone over the dark shadow of the world, and now some part of them crave pain, blood and violence... whatever form of violence it takes. So you have to confront things like the Gangrel increasingly going abruptly afk after rage burst; or the Mekhet getting herself lost in the symbolic pattern of those little animals that buzz and fly over some points in the map but no others, that suspicious three lane shape; the Daeva forgetting about teamplay and wandering alone because of the bloodlust and caring only about her feelings when achieving the kill, the Nosferatu's tactics going wrong -too hard, too good- and backlashing into permanent bans; the Ventrue refusing to acknowledge her faults and trying to control her team to the point of dictatorial play. <br /> <br /> Anyway, thank you for this amusing chat. Has been funny to think of Vampires in LoL for once, anyway. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br /> <br /> PS. Know what? Werewolves. With their pack instinct, now THOSE would be fine League of Legends players. xD<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Predators tend to follow prey. It's not like human migration patterns involved us just marching toward new land. It's a slow thing. We settled somewhere, we spread out, and the population just sort of drifted over the landscape. Easily slow enough for vampires to follow.</div> </div> </div> </div>+1 to this. In B&amp;S it is heavily implied that vampirism is as old as humanity herself, so they have had plenty of time to get to the Americas, following humans trails. A different thing is that (of course, like happened too with humans) Old Continent vampires didn't expect to find another vampires there when they arrived.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">That aside, they might have just walked along the seabed. Deep underwater where there's no sun is where vamps can move with the least hassle.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't think so,if i must be honest. As vampires they could travel in the darkness, for sure, and maybe -but is a BIG maybe, i wouldn't allow it without some potent Devotion- withstand the water pressure. But what of the lack of blood? That travel could easily take three months (or more; Cristopher Columbus apparently spent between two months and one and some days in each travel... and he had boats!) of swimming/walking time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Userhat Aegpts Vlrs. </span><span style=\"color:#996633\">Coren &quot;Ojos de Fuego&quot;. </span><span style=\"color:#666699\">Mithrael. </span><span style=\"color:#ffcc00\">Menehet.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1349-phersus","name":"Phersus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1349_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T04:16:58-07:00","id":536792,"title":"","content":"I was assuming they could drink the fishes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T05:32:44-07:00","id":536850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Phersus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=536789#post536789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But let's not forget you're talking about vampires: you have gone and thought about the good things (well, not exactly good in that chat bothering Noseratu... let's say you've focused on the ways being a vampire would improve their playstyle). But now, you have to go back and think about all the wrongness they would inherit, too.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is very true; my own optimistic subjectivity is showing. In my other examples, I hinted at the negatives. Indeed, I wanted to convey each clan beast as a form of hyper-bias. The players improve in the ways mentioned because they are paying attention differently as an affect of the Beast. So the negative side of that is most welcome and a lovely microcosm of the theme of vampirism. The Venture can now better help his team coordinate but finds his ability to communicate that respectfully has left him. Bard - I mean, the Mekhet - can see every possible enemy trap before it is sprung but is too preoccupied with minutia that he fails to remain competitive, now playing a game all of his own. Though the world through a Vampire's eyes reveals much, it conceals as well.<br /> <br /> Which as a side-note, brings up an interesting thought. As high humanity is 'good', it be tempting to model it that you get these negatives when it falls but keep the benefits no matter what, encouraging high humanity play. However, given that these senses are the Beast's doing, perhaps the political buff for the Venture or the perception buff for the Mekhet is dependent on and strengthened by a failing humanity. Which means that not only are neonates that cling to humanity naive, they're outclassed! (without serious self-improvement to give the Man a similar edge. Machiavelli needn't have being a vampire.)<br /> <br /> And I approve of Werewolf league players. However, as losses would result in destroyed hardware, I wouldn't recommend it without sponsorship.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2003,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","user":"Tabanese","date":".  spelling and what-not. 06-02-2015, 08:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T10:21:56-07:00","id":537187,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=536659#post536659\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not like human migration patterns involved us just marching toward new land. It's a slow thing. We settled somewhere, we spread out, and the population just sort of drifted over the landscape. Easily slow enough for vampires to follow.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's... actually a really good point. I suppose it wouldn't necessarily have been any more difficult for vampires to follow humans into the Americas than it would have been to follow them anywhere else.<br /> <br /> Of course, we can't be certain exactly how old the clans are, but the fact that at least some of them were able to survive among relatively small groups hunter-gatherers (as shown by their presence in precolonial California, for instance) suggests that it might have been possible for them to follow early human migrations, despite the obvious difficulties involved.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T11:27:17-07:00","id":537232,"title":"","content":"Remember that most of the more mobile vampires tend to be the younger ones than the established elders - so, we also have vampires with not only higher humanity, but lower BP doing the traveling. Not only less threat from sunlight, but the ability to feed from animals as the need rises.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T12:38:58-07:00","id":537321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=536659#post536659\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That aside, they might have just walked along the seabed. Deep underwater where there's no sun is where vamps can move with the least hassle.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except there are trenches located along the bottom of the oceans, deep ocean currents to wash them away and the inability to reference the position of the sun and stars to navigate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T13:16:28-07:00","id":537369,"title":"","content":"Well, in this scenario they wouldn't have known the Americas existed, so getting lost and randomly wandering ashore there is kind of what you'd expect to have happened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T18:41:07-07:00","id":537595,"title":"","content":"For getting to the Americas, I imagine they'd have followed the land bridge across the Artic circle. Isn't that how humans came to the Americas in the first place? Plus, there's a long time when the sun won't even shine down across that area during the winter, making travel even easier.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T01:24:35-07:00","id":537882,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=537595#post537595\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For getting to the Americas, I imagine they'd have followed the land bridge across the Artic circle. Isn't that how humans came to the Americas in the first place? Plus, there's a long time when the sun won't even shine down across that area during the winter, making travel even easier.</div> </div> </div> </div>They could have; the only question is whether most or all of the clans were present in Beringia (the sub-continent that used to occupy much of what's now the Bering Sea) more than ten thousand years ago. There were almost certainly subsequent migrations of varying sizes over the ensuing millennia, and the Aleuts and neighboring peoples were quite capable of island-hopping from Siberia to Alaska, so it's possible some or all of the clans arrived at some later date.<br /> <br /> As for crossing the ocean floor, I think the abyssal plain is covered in a thick layer of fine sediment that would be really hard to walk through, and the oceans are vast - a vampire who got lost might very well never find their way back to land. Crossing the Bering Sea might be easier, since it's actually part of the North American plate, and a lot shallower than the open ocean. Plus, you could follow the underwater slopes of the Aleutian islands to avoid getting lost. Still, I think most kindred would prefer a kayak. :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 06-03-2015, 04:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T04:14:53-07:00","id":537989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=536792#post536792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was assuming they could drink the fishes.</div> </div> </div> </div> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana\">There are some serious problems with that option. I'll try to number them here:</span><br /> <br /> <u><b>First: Pressure.</b></u><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> From Europe to America kindred would have to travel trough the Abyssopelagic Zone, from</span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> 4000 meters (13,100 feet) to 6000 meters (19,600 feet) deep, which means a pressure from </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><u><b>400 to 600</b></u></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> atmospheres (in the Hadalpelagic Zone, in deep water trenches and canyons is even worse, reaching higher and higher pressures until eight tons/square inch in the oceans deepest point: the Mariana Trench. But we'll forget about that). The water temperature is near freezing, and there is no light at all.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">Even in the surface, where a bipedal body can move with -somewhat- speed and accuracy, fishing is a daunting perspective. Moving and hunting at 500 atmospheres of pressure? Absolutely no way, but for the very, VERY old ones. But we'll talk about age later.</span></span><br /> <br /> <u><b>Second: Blood.</b></u><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> There is plenty of life at the bottom of the oceans, yes, but most of it is microscopical and/or invertebrates (starfish, tube worms), whose body fluids i wouldn't consider fit for a vampire (even young ones) without a specific devotion at least. Try to think about drinking insects... and even that comparison would be generous.</span><br /> <br /> <u><b>Third: Time of travel.</b></u><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> I was being naive, thinking of only three months. I was assuming it would be like a normal walk in the surface. Vampires don't need to rest, but the pressure would make each step a monumental challenge... if possible at all. And also what points Tsusasi: trenches? Deep currents? Add more and more time to the travel, even if they manage to surmount those obstacles. And time, in such an inhospitable environement, is crucial for survival: the less time you spend there, the likelier is you'll be coming out alive.</span><br /> <br /> <u><b>Fourth: Age.</b></u><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> MCN brings up that low-blooded potency vampires are more mobile and able to feed on animals than elder ones. He's right. But what they have in flexibility, they lack in raw power. Crussing pressure? No Vigor enough for moving, no Resilience enough to resist it. No specialized powers to travel. Too little blood supply to hang on when hunting goes badly... and that should be the rule, not the exception, in such an alien environment.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">All those things considered, i think walking the oceans bottom is out of the table for vampires trying to reach another continents. But we're not out of solutions here. Let's look to another perspective. Not walk, but swim: swimming all the trip, standing at just the right depth that allow the vampire to flee the light, but high enough to dodge the crushing pressure.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">We're talking then about traveling through the Mesopelagic / Bathypelagic Zones.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> The Mesopelagic Zone lays just below the Epipelagic Zone (where there is light from the sun), and extends</span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> from 200 meters (650 feet) to 1000 meters (3200 feet). It is called the twilight zone, because the light that penetrates to this depth is extremely faint. It is in this zone where the strange and bizarre bioluminiscent fish dwell. If their blood is able to sustain a vampire or not, i leave it at ST discretion. Keep in mind, we're still talking about pressures from 20 to 100 atmospheres. That's insane.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"> The next zone, is the Bathypelagic. It is called \u201cthe midnight zone\u201d or \u201cthe dark zone\u201d. It goes from 1000 meters (3300 feet) down to 4000 meters (13,100 feet). As its names suggests, there's no light here, but which is produced by the creatures themselves. The water pressure at this depth start ressembling that of the Abysoplegic Zone, reaching 5,800 pounds per square inch. There are large number of animales here in spite of that (Sperm whales can dive down to this level in search of food, giant squids live here too). The problem then, comes not only from facing the crusing pressure, but also the occasional predator... much bigger, much meaner, much more agile than any bipedal body. The feed problem could be solved by Animalism. But not every vampire has it, and not everytime it would solve anything: there are ENORMOUS areas in the sea without any fish presence.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">[Speaking of disciplines and everything else: there's one clan that could attempt such travel swimming, and of course i'm talking about the Gangrel. But such as i see it, again only specialized ones and old (and weird) enough to know how to... and we were talking about all vampires, regardless of clan.]</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">So, all in all, let's say we're travelling through the Twilight Zone,as is the only one suited more or less to hunt and avoid the sun. The chances of survival depend on two factors: luck and time. Luck to find prey (and to hunt them; they are adapted to the pressure and swimming; our average young vampire is neither) and time (before something goes awfully wrong \u2013 a current, a fight, most probably a loss of direction (everything is dark blue there without solid points of reference, you can easily swin in circles)-). Definitely not a good idea.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">TL,DR: If you're not a gangrel heavily specialized in marine adaptations in Protean, don't try to swin between continents. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></span><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Userhat Aegpts Vlrs. </span><span style=\"color:#996633\">Coren &quot;Ojos de Fuego&quot;. </span><span style=\"color:#666699\">Mithrael. </span><span style=\"color:#ffcc00\">Menehet.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1349-phersus","name":"Phersus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1349_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T04:52:56-07:00","id":538075,"title":"","content":"You don't need to be that old to master vigor or resilience. You stay able to drink animal blood for a century. That's more than enough time to get them maxed out if you put your mind to it. <br /> <br /> Also, torpor. In 2e losing blood potency doesn't drop any attributes you got above the cap. So you could theoretically have an elder come out of a power nap weak enough to drink shark <i>and</i> strong/fast enough to catch them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T05:40:38-07:00","id":538086,"title":"","content":"Riding the currents in a casket in a torpor seems far more likely than actively swimming it.<br /> <br /> Spontaneous embraces in the Americas seem most likely of all. I mean, there are over a dozen myths/legends/stories of spontaneous embraces in Europe in the core book. Why wouldn't other continents show similar numbers?<br /> <br /> I don't see vampires migrating anywhere until travel is already made fairly safe between the two points. Carriages. Ships. Vehicles that provide shelter in the daylight, unless you have a dot in Protean. This would both be for safety (it would take dire circumstances for a vampire to leave a city and risk the sun without reliable transport) and food purposes. Hunting among a small few settlers could go very bad very quickly. Illnesses, raids, not enough blood to feed from, maybe even just loneliness from not having any other vampires around.<br /> <br /> There are, of course, always exceptional circumstances, and it's ultimately up to the ST, but I think the option that requires the least leaps of faith would be spontaneous embraces, if you want them to exist before colonization began in earnest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T06:27:01-07:00","id":538197,"title":"","content":"Unmarked Grave being the <i>first</i> dot of protean now does mean that a lot more vampires will know it than they otherwise would.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T09:02:57-07:00","id":538464,"title":"","content":"No it doesn't. Protean is still a clan specific discipline, which requires it to be taught and carries the risk of vinculum and gaining the Addicted condition to learn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T09:30:31-07:00","id":538486,"title":"","content":"Yeah, but it takes less effort to learn the first dot than the second or third (which the ability has previously sat at in other versions).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T10:47:01-07:00","id":538538,"title":"","content":"Is sea pressure that much of a danger to something that can't drown, and can therefore keep its mouth &amp; nose open to let in water to equalize the pressure? I get that movement would be vastly slowed when swimming or walking through fluid that's effectively denser than mercury, but I'm skeptical about pressure causing harm rather than inconvenience.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T10:51:28-07:00","id":538542,"title":"","content":"I'd say it definitely would be. A vampire can still take physical damage from a beating, a baseball bat, a tossed bowling ball etc, and all that is, is pressure being applied to the point of contact. At sufficient depth (someone who knows the maths better then me can probably say how deep), I would say Vampires would take damage from the pressure being exerted on their physical forms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5439-gullinbursti","name":"Gullinbursti","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5439_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"291"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T10:58:45-07:00","id":538553,"title":"","content":"A shark would take some of the same damage from beatings and such, and yet they can swim damned deep with little regard to the depths they swim at. Same could be said of other aquatic creatures. And then you have whales who dive really deep too, who do NOT equalize pressure. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Yes, EVENTUALLY pressure will be a concern, but I think it is being vastly overrated here for Vampires. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":540,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","user":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","date":". 06-03-2015, 02:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T10:59:10-07:00","id":538554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gullinbursti</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=538542#post538542\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say it definitely would be. A vampire can still take physical damage from a beating, a baseball bat, a tossed bowling ball etc, and all that is, is pressure being applied to the point of contact. At sufficient depth (someone who knows the maths better then me can probably say how deep), I would say Vampires would take damage from the pressure being exerted on their physical forms.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I think what he meant is - let in the water to fill up your body. So your body has equal pressure from both sides and you wouldn't get crushed under it's weight. Not sure if it's feasable tho. We need someone with some science/medicine dots to answer the question :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B5_EJSgV1u4SOFFJc3M5VmFDWVk?resourcekey=0-GEErNnDbGqlKYgoiuGausw&amp;usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Bloodline conversions</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1IbeKfqhWuG8jPN9vpGmDo0VY9n9-4ejxvRzfJRoir4k/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My House rules</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/294-griautis","name":"Griautis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar294_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T11:15:58-07:00","id":538574,"title":"","content":"Hm, well reading, it seems that movement would be dependent on depth (and a counter balance of strength dots), while pressure really depends on Vampire physiology within the World of Darkness. If there is no oxygen within the vampire and they can fully allow water to flood all their body cavities so there is no air within them, pressure isn't a problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5439-gullinbursti","name":"Gullinbursti","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5439_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"291"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T12:43:56-07:00","id":538682,"title":"","content":"Whales, sharks, squid, dolphins etc. do die from self inflicted decompression sickness, so ignoring how far down they go and for how long can be fatal even to creatures who spend their entire lives underwater. Eventually the pressure is going to crush their brain and heart.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T12:56:49-07:00","id":538687,"title":"","content":"Yes, that is true, but at the depths they swim, they don't tend to worry about it (and <i><b>decompression sickness </b></i>really only applies to air breathers, <i><b>for the most part</b></i>, because they went too deep for too long, which a vampire, who does not actually breathe, does not care about). I would say that a Vampire can easily survive at those depths.<br /> <br /> And again, you are talking about crushing organs a Vampire really does not care about all that much. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T13:24:02-07:00","id":538712,"title":"","content":"Well, dammit, effectively as a vampire you would be able to open you mouth, eventually allowing your stomach, lungs, intestines, ears... become completely filled with water. I don't know how i overlooked it. I got caught in normal physiology rather than undead one. But wait, let's do this in a different way: I'm going to do a little break from vampires. I'll explain things from human side, then return to vampires, because physiology is fun.<br /> <br /> The real problem about pressure is the gas in the human body (that, by the way, answers your question regarding sharks and fishes and pressure, Tymeaus; i'll speak about whales later): The air in our lungs is what would compress and kill us (and the fact that oxygen becomes toxic for us under pressure, as it increases its effects). But putting that gas aside, our body <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">is filled with chambers and channels that carry fluids -- not only blood, but lympha and cerebrospinal (CSF) fluid aswell, for example. As the pressure builds, our liquid-filled tissues wouldn't collapse, because liquids can withstand higher pressures and not shrink in volume, but the thing is our tissues aren't designed for that pressure, so they would eventually rupture at some point. And this point is what's cool, because it leads me to the question: <u>what would happen inside our head?</u> Our brain is closed in a tight box made of stone, with our cerebellum and brain inside, kept at the same pressure thanks to that tightness and the CSF aswell. Any little crack (or scar tissue, less resistant than bone) in those bones (bones that have empty little spaces inside them, by the way) could lead to a dramatic end. (Also, there are another mecial problemas such as aseptic bone necrosis due to circulatory failure in the capillaries within our bones, but those stuff take more time and aren't as funny as the previous).</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">The real problem about pressure, moving backwards to vampires, would be the strength to move, then, i suppose! <br /> <br /> About whales, Tymeaus: the problem about them is the oxygen (again). The thing is, not only their ribs are bound by bendable cartigale (like ours, but much more flexible) that allow their rib cage to collapse when the pressure rises: their lung can do that aswell, preventing them thusly from rupturing AND blocking gas exchange in the lung. Lack of nitrogen absortion prevents the development of decompression sickness. In this stage, deep dive whales turn to the oxygen stored in their blood and muscle to keep on their metabolism.</span></span><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Userhat Aegpts Vlrs. </span><span style=\"color:#996633\">Coren &quot;Ojos de Fuego&quot;. </span><span style=\"color:#666699\">Mithrael. </span><span style=\"color:#ffcc00\">Menehet.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1349-phersus","name":"Phersus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1349_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T06:01:34-07:00","id":539464,"title":"","content":"Learn something new everyday... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T11:57:38-07:00","id":539779,"title":"","content":"Phersus, please educate us on all the things you know. That was pretty awesome.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T12:36:08-07:00","id":539825,"title":"","content":"Oh, and if there is some spot in World of Darkness that drops down to 22+ miles below sea level (maybe an avernian gate in the Mariana Trench), then apparently you would risk the bones being crushed by the pressure surrounding you. (Not sure how accurate that was, but it looked like a legit site and legit mathematics <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5439-gullinbursti","name":"Gullinbursti","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5439_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"291"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T13:05:27-07:00","id":539881,"title":"","content":"You know, I'm pretty sure vamps can swim.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T13:10:48-07:00","id":539898,"title":"","content":"Gulp in air, don't exhale, serve as a flotation device! Arrrr, the life of a pirate vampire be a depressing one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5439-gullinbursti","name":"Gullinbursti","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5439_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"291"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T13:18:50-07:00","id":539907,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/531432-a-new-perspective?p=539881#post539881\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know, I'm pretty sure vamps can swim.</div> </div> </div> </div>The question may instead be: do vampires get exhausted while swimming?<br /> <br /> Given that they still have, use, and improve their Stamina, I'd say probably yes. Not sure what the metaphysics behind that are, but there we go.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T13:29:10-07:00","id":539931,"title":"","content":"Rose did answer that before, but about running in general: They can briefly experience exhaustion, but don't get fatigued like humans do. Give them a moment to 'catch their breath' and they're ready to get right back to it. Scary thought.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T14:19:37-07:00","id":539969,"title":"","content":"Can you link that? I'd like to see the reasoning behind it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T14:22:24-07:00","id":539972,"title":"","content":"There isn't reasoning, insofar that it isn't something to be reasoned. Vampires can tire briefly but don't suffer long term exhaustion. She was just clarifying rather than justifying.<br /> <br /> I'll look for the post, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T14:26:39-07:00","id":539976,"title":"","content":"It's okay; I'll be grateful if you find it but I was only interested in why, so you don't have to get it. Little tidbits like that go a long way I find when constructing Vampire metaphysics. :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T07:23:09-07:00","id":541146,"title":"","content":"Thank you for the kind words, Leetsepeak, but i'm just your average medicine student. The stuff about whales is because well, i'm an absolute nerd when it comes to animals (specially the predatory-type acuatic ones).<br /> <br /> About swimming and vamps: yes, i'd definitely allow them to swim. Because basically, the only things you need to do it is just move with enough strenght and brain-muscle coordination to compensate for your weight, (and taking into account the density that has the liquid you're simming through). To achieve it, there are two major types of subacuatic strategies: those that involve some sort of passive propulsion in the vertical axis (like a gas bladder, or by storing lipids -or even oils-) and those (like sharks) that use dynamic lift. Basically, that means that sharks use their pectoral fins in an airplane-like manner: as they swim, those fins create lift who allows the fish to maintain (or modify) depth. <span style=\"font-size:10px\">(The major problems of this method is that if you stop swimming... you stop being afloat. And that's the reason sharks are incapable of swimming backwards)</span><br /> <br /> As we (and therefore vampires) have not gas bladder, we have maintain ourselves by swimming constantly... exactly like sharks. So yeah, vampires could very much swim without problem. <br /> <br /> About resilience: i was considering vampires didn't experience exhaustion at all, as it comes from the muscle need for oxygen and nutrients, and vamps run on the magical power of vitae. But well, that's one thing houseruled easily taking into account the Stamina trait, as Maina says. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Userhat Aegpts Vlrs. </span><span style=\"color:#996633\">Coren &quot;Ojos de Fuego&quot;. </span><span style=\"color:#666699\">Mithrael. </span><span style=\"color:#ffcc00\">Menehet.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1349-phersus","name":"Phersus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1349_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}